From: Inigo Montoya <inigo@u.washington.edu>
Subject: PPL essay and sundry

Michael R Linksvayer <mrl28033@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu> makes some
excellent points.

>Legislation and law production are not equivalent. 

Correct.  Neither are taxation and the collection of premiums/fees
by protection agencies, a parallel implicit later in the sentence.  
However, to clarify this, I added "and adjudication" to the parenthetical 
"legislation".

Your comments on the sections on defense and the environment were 
excellent -- I incorporated as much as I could.  They were remarkably 
skinny sections, anyway.  :-)  Thanks!

You're the second person to mention _Across Realtime_ (which I have
a copy of) as the best way to get the story and the novel -- I've
added that, including ISBN number.  If someone has a copy of
Benson's book, and could email me the ISBN number/publishing info,
I'll add that (otherwise, I'll go grep around in a few online library
catalogs) information as well (I have the info for Friedman).  I
attempted to keep the recommended reading referred to as readily
available as possible (ha!  not very, more's the pity), so I will
likely not included references to Extropy/Extropians-list/etc.
(unless someone convinces me that would in fact be a good idea).

Hal Finney hal@alumni.caltech.edu writes:

>Law could be "made" in several different ways in a PPL system.  Perhaps
>one way is, as Michael Linksvayer says, through court decisions.  ...

>Another possibility is for a protection agency to simply invent a legal
>code which it offers to enforce for its clients. ...
>Likewise, in a PPL system, societal forces will tend to 
>determine what laws are in widespread use.  But the individual legal 
>agencies have to set their own prices and codes of law.

[In an effort to minimize quoting, I chopped some stuff out.  I
apologize if something critical got left out.]

It seems unlikely to me that health/fire/auto/you-name-it insurance
will disappear under PPL.  As I suggested in my essay, insurance companies
are, if anything, a good representation in the here-and-now of what
protection agencies might be like under PPL.  Likely everyone here is
familiar with "law-making" (or at least, rule making) as practiced by
insurance companies.  So you want to drive a car?  If you smoke, we'll
charge you more because you're statistically more of a risk -- a de
facto punishment for smoking, applying at least some pressure to quit.
Want life insurance?  Do you skydive or engage in certain other 
hazardous sports?  Why don't you try the company down the street.  They'll
probably insure you -- but it'll cost you.

I would be very surprised if no protection agency offered this kind
of variable scale/coverage.  If a company wants to inspect your home/place
of business before insuring you for fire, and requires installation and
maintenance of smoke detectors and fire extinguishers, very likely 
a company might want you to arm yourself and regularly train using
that weapon, before they "insure" you against attack.  And another
company, with a different set of opinions, might charge you more if
you choose to go armed.

While no one is going to use force to coerce you to follow this
"rule", while this is certainly even weaker than the "law" created
and enforced by protection agencies, I think this sort of regulation
would have a profound impact on an anarcho-capitalist society.

It is, I think, probable that when signing on with a protection agency,
one will discover in the fine print a whole batch of regulations
involving the environment/national defense/problem-of-the-week that
one had no desire to pay for, included in an otherwise discounted
package.  Don't want those?  OK.  Tell us what you want.  And it
winds up costing 2x, or more.

Would it be worth it, to write your own ticket?  Depends.  Maybe
this is one way of getting a public good that people won't 
otherwise pay for.  Maybe it'll never actually happen.  The only
ways I see to get the extra money to discount and finance a public good
are (a) lowered transaction costs associated with a "standard"
contract (b) the public good, if enough people go for it, does pay
for itself, plus a bit, even if it loses money right up to that
critical point (c) all custom package prices are inflated.  If (c),
you risk your customer stalking out and finding someone who *doesn't*
inflate their prices.  And if that someone doesn't exist, becoming
that someone.


